/*!
    \page qtbridges-python-future.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Python Future Plans
    \brief Qt Bridge Python Future Plans

    This document outlines the planned features and improvements for QtBridges.

    \section1 1. Table Data Support

    \b {Description:} Native support for tabular data with Pandas DataFrames
    and SQLite databases.

    \b {Features:}
    \list
        \li Automatic column detection and role mapping for DataFrames
        \li Built-in sorting, filtering, and pagination
        \li Direct SQLite query result integration
        \li Efficient data updates without full model resets
    \endlist

    \section1 2. QML Type Generation Tool: \c {generate_qmltypes}

    \b {Description:} Command-line tool to generate \c {.qmltypes} files for
    Python projects, providing IDE auto-completion and type checking in QML.

    \b {Benefit:} Better IDE support (Qt Creator, VS Code) with accurate
    auto-completion and error checking for Python-backed QML types.

    \section1 3. Smart QRangeModel Wrapper

    \b {Description:} Make \c {QRangeModel} (used by \c {bridge_instance()} for
    containers) behave like the original Python container with full attribute
    and method access.

    \b {Current Limitation:} QRangeModel is a snapshot; modifications don't
    sync with the original container.

    \section1 4. Resolve QRangeModel Limitations

    \b {Description:} Address all issues documented in \c {limitations.md}

    \section1 5. Complex Type Support

    \b {Description:} Full support for nested and complex Python types as
    documented in \c {limitations.md}:
    \list
        \li Nested lists, dicts, and custom objects
        \li Proper conversion and lifetime management
        \li QML access to nested properties
    \endlist

    \section1 6. Free Function Exposure: \b {bridge_function()}

    \b {Description:} Expose standalone Python functions to QML as global
    functions or singleton methods.

    \section1 7. Data Visualization Examples

    \b {Description:} Comprehensive examples demonstrating QtBridges with data
    visualization.

    \section1 8. Deployment Tool: \c {qtbridges-deploy}

    \b {Description:} Deployment tool based on Nuitka (similar to \c
    {pyside6-deploy}) for packaging QtBridges applications into standalone
    executables.

    \section1 9. Associative Container Support

    \b {Description:} Extend \c {bridge_instance()} to support Python
    dictionaries and sets in addition to lists and tuples.

    \b {Dictionary Example:}

    \code
    user_data = {
        "name": "Alice",
        "age": 30,
        "email": "alice@example.com"
    }

    bridge_instance(user_data, name="UserData", uri="backend")
    \endcode

    \code
    import backend 1.0

    Text {
        text: UserData.name  // Key-based access
    }
    \endcode

    \b {Set Example:}

    \code
    active_ids = {101, 102, 103}
    bridge_instance(active_ids, name="ActiveIDs", uri="backend")
    \endcode

    \section1 10. Additional Enhancements

    \section2 10.1. Custom Signal Support: \c {@signal}

    \b {Description:} Allow Python classes to declare custom signals for
    event-driven communication with QML.

    \b {Example:}

    \code
    from QtBridge import signal, bridge_type

    class DataLoader:
        @signal
        def loading_started(self):
            pass

        @signal
        def loading_finished(self, success: bool):
            pass

        def load_data(self):
            self.loading_started.emit()
            # ... load data ...
            self.loading_finished.emit(True)
    \endcode

    \section2 10.2. Python-to-QML Function Calls

    \b {Note:} This feature may be redundant if custom signal support (see item
    12) is fully implemented. However, enabling direct Python-to-QML function
    calls is a simpler approach and could provide immediate benefits.

    \b {Description:} Enable Python code to directly call QML functions and
    trigger property change signals when properties are modified from Python.

    \b {Current Limitation:} QtBridges only supports QML→Python direction.
    Property change signals only emit when properties are set from QML, not
    from Python. No direct way to call QML functions.

    \b {Benefit:} Full bidirectional communication between Python and QML
    without requiring PySide6's \c {QMetaObject.invokeMethod()} or manual
    signal wiring.

    \section2 10.3. IDE Integration

    \b {Description:} VS Code and PyCharm plugins for:
    \list
        \li Auto-completion for Python-backed QML types
        \li Navigation between Python and QML code
        \li Type checking and error highlighting
        \li Refactoring support across languages
    \endlist
*/

